ses,\n            \"val_mae_losses\": val_mae_losses,\n\n            \"seed\": SEED,\n            \"input_size\": (512, 512),\n            \"gt_downsample\": 4,\n            \"sigma\": 5,\n            \"architecture\": \"FourColumnMCNN\",\n            \"training_dataset\": \"ShanghaiTech Part B\"\n        },\n        LATEST_MODEL_PATH\n    )\n\n    # SAVE TRAINING HISTORY TO CSV\n    history_df = pd.DataFrame(\n        {\n            \"epoch\": np.arange(1, len(train_losses) + 1),\n            \n            \"train_loss\": train_losses,\n            \"val_loss\": val_losses,\n\n            \"train_mae\": train_mae_losses,\n            \"val_mae\": val_mae_losses\n        }\n    )\n\n    history_df[\"is_best_epoch\"] = (history_df[\"epoch\"] == best_epoch + 1)\n    history_df.to_csv(HISTORY_CSV_PATH, index=False)\n\n    # EARLY STOPPING\n    if epochs_without_improvement >= PATIENCE:\n        print(\"Early stopping triggered.\")\n        print(f\"No validation improvement for {PATIENCE} epochs.\")\n        break","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T19:32:31.2288Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T19:32:31.2291Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.692741Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T19:32:31.229067Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.691897Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Training\n## `model.train()`\n\nAt the beginning of training:\n\n```python\nmodel.train()\n```\n\nThis tells PyTorch:\n\n> The model is currently being trained.\n\nThis matters especially for layers such as BatchNorm.\n\n## Batch processing\n\nFor every batch:\n\n```python\nfor images, density_maps, gt_counts in train_loader:\n```\n\nyou get:\n\n```text\nimages\ndensity_maps\ngt_counts\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nimages:\n[8,3,768,768]\n\ndensity maps:\n[8,1,192,192]\n\ncounts:\n[8]\n```\n\n## Move data to GPU\n\nAll tensors must be on the same device as the model.\n\nPyTorch generally cannot perform an operation between CPU tensors and GPU tensors. Therefore, i move the batch to same device.\n\n## Clear previous gradients\n\nPyTorch accumulates gradients by default.\n\nTherefore, before calculating gradients for a new batch, i clear the previous gradients.\n\n## Forward pass\n\n```python\npredictions = model(images)\n```\n\nThis performs:\n\n```text\nImages\n ↓\nColumn 1\nColumn 2\nColumn 3\nColumn 4\n ↓\nFusion\n ↓\nDensity map\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\npredictions\n```\n\n## Calculate loss\n\n```python\nloss, count_mae = criterion(\n    predictions,\n    density_maps,\n    gt_counts\n)\n```\n\nThis produces:\n\n```text\ncombined loss\n+\ncount MAE\n```\n\n## Backpropagation\n\n`loss` is actually a PyTorch Tensor.\n\nConceptually:\n```\nloss = combined_loss\n```\nAnd PyTorch Tensor objects have a `.backward()` method.\n\nThis calculates gradients(in `model`, generated during `model.train()`).\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nLoss\n ↓\nHow did each parameter contribute?\n ↓\nGradient\n```\n\nThe gradient tells the optimizer which direction to change each parameter.\n\n## Gradient clipping\n\n```python\ntorch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(\n    model.parameters(),\n    max_norm=5.0\n)\n```\n\nThis limits the gradient norm.\n\nWhy?\n\nSometimes gradients can become extremely large.\n\nThis is called:\n\n> exploding gradients\n\nGradient clipping prevents updates from becoming excessively large.\n\n## Optimizer update\n\nThen:\n\n```python\noptimizer.step()\n```\n\nAdam updates the network weights.\n\nSo the full training sequence is:\n\n```text\nForward\n   ↓\nLoss\n   ↓\nBackward\n   ↓\nGradient clipping\n   ↓\nAdam update\n```\n\nThis is repeated for every batch.\n\n## Accumulating training metrics\n\nThe code does:\n\n```python\nbatch_size_current = images.size(0)\n```\n```\n[batch_size, channels, height, width]\n     ↑\n   size(0)\n```\n\n`.item()` extracts the ordinary Python number:\n\n```python\ntrain_loss_total += (loss.item() * batch_size_current)\ntrain_mae_total += (count_mae.item() * batch_size_current)\n```\n\n**Why multiply by batch size?**\n\nSuppose you have 3 batches:\n\n```\nBatch 1 → 10 images → loss = 2\nBatch 2 → 10 images → loss = 4\nBatch 3 →  5 images → loss = 8\n```\n\nIf you simply did:\n```\ntotal += loss\n```\nyou would get:\n```\n2 + 4 + 8 = 14\n```\nand average by number of batches:\n```\n14 / 3 = 4.67\n```\nBut that's not the true per-image average, because the last batch has only 5 images.\n\nInstead, multiply each batch's loss by its number of samples:\n```\nBatch 1: 2 × 10 = 20\nBatch 2: 4 × 10 = 40\nBatch 3: 8 ×  5 = 40\n```\nTotal:\n```\n20 + 40 + 40 = 100\n```\nTotal images:\n```\n10 + 10 + 5 = 25\n```\nAverage:\n```\n100 / 25 = 4\n```\nThat's the proper dataset-level average.\n\nThis collects the loss over all batches.\n\nAt the end:\n\n```python\ntrain_loss =\ntrain_loss_total / len(train_loader.dataset)\n```\n\nSo i get the average training loss.\n\n## Validation phase\n\nAfter training batches finish:\n\n```python\nmodel.eval()\n```\n\nThis switches the model into evaluation mode.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\nwith torch.inference_mode():\n```\n\ndisables gradient tracking.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause we aren't updating the model during validation.\n\nThis saves memory and computation.\n\n**Why validation shouldn't update weights**\n\nIf you trained on the validation data, the validation set would no longer be an independent measure of how well your model generalizes.\n\n## Validation forward pass\n\nValidation does:\n\n```text\nvalidation image\n       ↓\nmodel\n       ↓\ndensity prediction\n       ↓\ncombined loss\n       ↓\nvalidation metrics\n```\n\nBut importantly:\n\n```text\nNO backward()\nNO optimizer.step()\n```\n\nSo the model isn't trained on validation data.\n\n## Training vs validation\n\nYour loop is essentially:\n\n```text\nTRAIN\n ├─ model.train()\n ├─ forward\n ├─ loss\n ├─ backward\n ├─ optimizer.step()\n │\n └───────────────┐\n                 ▼\nVALIDATION\n ├─ model.eval()\n ├─ inference\n ├─ loss\n └─ metrics\n```\n\n## Learning-rate update\n\nAfter validation:\n\n```python\nscheduler.step()\n```\n\nSince i'm using `StepLR`, the learning rate decreases according to the fixed epoch schedule.\n\n## Recording history\n\nThe code stores:\n\n```python\ntrain_losses\nval_losses\ntrain_mae_losses\nval_mae_losses\n```\n\nThese become lists like:\n\n```text\ntrain_losses = [\n  0.81,\n  0.62,\n  0.54,\n  ...\n]\n```\n\n## Best validation model\n\nThis is the key condition:\n\n```python\nif val_loss < best_val_loss:\n```\n\nIf the current validation loss is better than everything before it:\n\n```text\nsave this model\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```python\nbest_val_loss = val_loss\n```\n\nand:\n\n```python\nbest_epoch = epoch\n```\n\n### What gets saved?\n\nThe checkpoint contains:\n\n```text\nepoch\nmodel_state_dict\noptimizer_state_dict\nbest_val_loss\ntrain_losses\nval_losses\ntrain_mae_losses\nval_mae_losses\nseed\ninput_size\ngt_downsample\nsigma\narchitecture\ntraining_dataset\n```\n\nThis is good because the checkpoint contains not only weights but also useful training metadata. \n\n### Why save optimizer state?\n\nSuppose training stops at:\n\n```text\nEpoch 45\n```\n\nIf you save only:\n\n```text\nmodel weights\n```\n\nyou can't fully reproduce the optimizer's state.\n\nSaving:\n\n```python\noptimizer_state_dict\n```\n\nallows training to potentially continue more faithfully.\n\n## Early stopping\n\nYour code tracks:\n\n```python\nepochs_without_improvement\n```\n\nEvery time validation loss improves:\n\n```text\ncounter = 0\n```\n\nOtherwise:\n\n```text\ncounter += 1\n```\n\nWhen:\n\n```text\ncounter >= 30\n```\n\ntraining stops.\n\n### Why early stopping?\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nEpoch 30: validation loss = 0.25\nEpoch 31: 0.26\nEpoch 32: 0.27\n...\n```\n\nThe training loss may continue decreasing while validation performance gets worse.\n\nThat can indicate:\n\n> overfitting\n\nEarly stopping prevents wasting computation and helps avoid training far beyond the point of useful generalization.\n\n### What does the model actually learn?\n\nThis is perhaps the most important conceptual question.\n\nThe model isn't explicitly told:\n\n```text\n\"There are 150 people.\"\n```\n\nonly.\n\nIt receives:\n\n```text\nImage\n+\ndensity map\n```\n\nand learns a function:\n\n$$\nf_\\theta(I)=\\hat D\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n* $I$ = input image\n* $\\theta$ = neural-network parameters\n* $\\hat D$ = predicted density map\n\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\hat C = \\sum_{x,y}\\hat D(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo the complete function is:\n\n$$\nI\n\\rightarrow\nf_\\theta(I)\n\\rightarrow\n\\hat D\n\\rightarrow\n\\sum\\hat D\n\\rightarrow\n\\hat C\n$$\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(\"TRAINING COMPLETE\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\n\nprint(f\"Best epoch:             {best_epoch + 1}\")\nprint(f\"Best validation loss:   {best_val_loss:.6f}\")\n\nif best_epoch >= 0:\n    print(f\"Best validation MAE:   {val_mae_losses[best_epoch]:.6f}\")\n\nprint(\"\\nBest checkpoint:\")\nprint(BEST_MODEL_PATH)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.69379Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.694148Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.699501Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.694125Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.69864Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"checkpoint = torch.load(BEST_MODEL_PATH, map_location=DEVICE)\n\nmodel = (FourColumnMCNN().to(DEVICE))\n\nmodel.load_state_dict(checkpoint[\"model_state_dict\"])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.700588Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.700911Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.778954Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.700882Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.778167Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"history_df.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.779799Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.780049Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.804825Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.780027Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.804213Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(15, 6))\n\n# Mark best epoch\nbest_rows = history_df[history_df[\"is_best_epoch\"]]\n\n# LOSS GRAPH\naxes[0].plot(\n    history_df[\"epoch\"],\n    history_df[\"train_loss\"],\n    label=\"Training Loss\",\n    linewidth=2\n)\n\naxes[0].plot(\n    history_df[\"epoch\"],\n    history_df[\"val_loss\"],\n    label=\"Validation Loss\",\n    linewidth=2\n)\n\nif not best_rows.empty:\n\n    best_epoch_plot = best_rows.iloc[0][\"epoch\"]\n\n    axes[0].axvline(\n        best_epoch_plot,\n        color=\"red\",\n        linestyle=\"--\",\n        linewidth=1.5,\n        alpha=0.7,\n        label=f\"Best Epoch ({int(best_epoch_plot)})\"\n    )\n\naxes[0].set_title(\"Training and Validation Loss\", fontsize=13, fontweight=\"bold\")\naxes[0].set_xlabel(\"Epoch\")\naxes[0].set_ylabel(\"Loss\")\naxes[0].legend()\naxes[0].grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# MAE GRAPH\naxes[1].plot(\n    history_df[\"epoch\"],\n    history_df[\"train_mae\"],\n    label=\"Training MAE\",\n    linewidth=2\n)\n\naxes[1].plot(\n    history_df[\"epoch\"],\n    history_df[\"val_mae\"],\n    label=\"Validation MAE\",\n    linewidth=2\n)\n\n# Mark best epoch\nif not best_rows.empty:\n    axes[1].axvline(\n        best_epoch_plot,\n        color=\"red\",\n        linestyle=\"--\",\n        linewidth=1.5,\n        alpha=0.7,\n        label=f\"Best Epoch ({int(best_epoch_plot)})\"\n    )\n\naxes[1].set_title(\"Training and Validation MAE\", fontsize=13, fontweight=\"bold\")\naxes[1].set_xlabel(\"Epoch\")\naxes[1].set_ylabel(\"MAE\")\naxes[1].legend()\naxes[1].grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n\n# FIGURE\nplt.suptitle(\"FourColumnMCNN Training Performance\", fontsize=16, fontweight=\"bold\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.805638Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.805908Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:39.2472Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:38.805886Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:39.246288Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Key Findings\n\n## Why MCNN is suitable for crowd counting\n\nCrowds have a scale problem.\n\nConsider:\n\n```text\nCamera near people\n\n████████████\n████████████\n```\n\nversus:\n\n```text\nCamera far away\n\n••••••••••••••••\n```\n\nPeople can have drastically different apparent sizes.\n\nYour network therefore has:\n\n```text\nLarge kernel branch\n        +\nmedium kernel branches\n        +\nsmall kernel branch\n```\n\nThis provides multi-scale feature extraction.\n\n## The most important mathematical formulation\n\nYour model can be described mathematically as follows.\n\nGiven an input image $I$:\n\n$$\n\\hat D=f_\\theta(I)\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\hat D\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H\\times W}\n$$\n\nis the predicted density map.\n\nThe predicted count is:\n\n$$\n\\hat C=\\sum_{x=1}^{H}\\sum_{y=1}^{W}\\hat D(x,y)\n$$\n\nThe density-map loss is:\n\n$$\nL_D=\n\\frac{1}{HW}\n\\sum_{x,y}\n(\\hat D(x,y)-D(x,y))^2\n$$\n\nThe counting loss is:\n\n$$\nL_C=|\\hat C-C|\n$$\n\nFor a batch, L1Loss averages these absolute differences.\n\nThe final objective is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\nL=0.9L_D+0.1L_C\n}\n$$\n\nThat equation summarizes one of the most important parts of your implementation.\n\n## Full end-to-end example\n\nSuppose an image contains:\n\n```text\nActual people = 50\n```\n\nThe annotation file contains:\n\n```text\n50 head points\n```\n\n### Step 1\n\nResize image:\n\n```text\noriginal\n   ↓\n768×768\n```\n\n### Step 2\n\nTransform 50 points to the resized coordinate system.\n\n### Step 3\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\n50 impulses\n```\n\n### Step 4\n\nApply Gaussian filtering.\n\n### Step 5\n\nNormalize:\n\n```text\nsum(density map) = 50\n```\n\n### Step 6\n\nDownsample:\n\n```text\n768×768\n ↓\n192×192\n```\n\nwhile compensating density values.\n\n### Step 7\n\nFeed image into MCNN.\n\n### Step 8\n\nFour branches extract features:\n\n```text\nlarge scale\nlarge-medium scale\nmedium scale\nsmall scale\n```\n\n### Step 9\n\nFeatures are concatenated:\n\n```text\n64 + 64 + 80 + 96 = 304 channels\n```\n\n### Step 10\n\nFusion produces:\n\n```text\n1-channel density map\n```\n\n### Step 11\n\nSuppose model predicts:\n\n```text\nsum(predicted density) = 47.8\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nPredicted count = 47.8\nActual count = 50\n```\n\nCount error:\n\n$$\n|47.8-50|=2.2\n$$\n\n### Step 12\n\nThe density-map MSE is also calculated.\n\n### Step 13\n\nFinal loss:\n\n$$\n0.9(MSE)+0.1(2.2)\n$$\n\n### Step 14\n\nBackpropagation calculates gradients.\n\n### Step 15\n\nAdam updates the model.\n\nAnd this repeats thousands of times.\n\n## What each major component is responsible for\n\n| Component                     | Purpose                                        |\n| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `EnhancedShanghaiTechDataset` | Loads/prepares training samples                |\n| `generate_density_map()`      | Converts point annotations into density maps   |\n| `CombinedLoss`                | Combines spatial and counting objectives       |\n| `FourColumnMCNN`              | Predicts density maps                          |\n| `Adam`                        | Updates model weights                          |\n| `StepLR`                      | Reduces learning rate                          |\n| `DataLoader`                  | Creates batches                                |\n| `validation`                  | Measures generalization                        |\n| `best checkpoint`             | Saves best-performing model                    |\n| `latest checkpoint`           | Saves latest training state                    |\n| `early stopping`              | Stops training after prolonged non-improvement |\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test_loss_total = 0.0\ntest_mae_total = 0.0\n\nwith torch.inference_mode():\n    progress_bar = tqdm(test_loader, desc=\"Testing\", leave=True)\n\n    for images, density_maps, gt_counts in progress_bar:\n\n        images = images.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n        density_maps = density_maps.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n        gt_counts = gt_counts.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n        \n        # Prediction\n        predictions = model(images)\n        loss, count_mae = criterion(predictions, density_maps, gt_counts)\n\n        # Accumulate Metrics\n        batch_size_current = images.size(0)\n        test_loss_total += (loss.item() * batch_size_current)\n        test_mae_total += (count_mae.item() * batch_size_current)\n\n        # Update Progress Bar\n        progress_bar.set_postfix(loss=f\"{loss.item():.4f}\", mae=f\"{count_mae.item():.2f}\")\n\n# Average Validation Metrics\ntest_loss = (test_loss_total / len(test_loader.dataset))\ntest_mae = (test_mae_total / len(test_loader.dataset))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:39.248238Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:39.248809Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.613489Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:39.248784Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.612421Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"=\" * 60)\nprint(\"TESTING COMPLETE\")\nprint(\"=\" * 60)\n\nprint(f\"{'Test loss:':<25} {test_loss:.6f}\")\nprint(f\"{'Test MAE:':<25} {test_mae:.4f}\")\n\nprint(\"-\" * 60)\n\nprint(\"Best checkpoint:\")\nprint(BEST_MODEL_PATH)\n\nprint(\"=\" * 60)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.614553Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.614806Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.620047Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.614784Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.619311Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def load_gt_boxes(label_path, width, height):\n\n    boxes = []\n\n    if not label_path.exists():\n        return boxes\n\n    with open(label_path, \"r\") as f:\n\n        for line in f:\n            values = line.strip().split()\n\n            if len(values) != 5:\n                continue\n\n            _, xc, yc, bw, bh = map(float, values)\n\n            # YOLO normalized -> pixels\n            xc *= width\n            yc *= height\n            bw *= width\n            bh *= height\n\n            x1 = xc - bw / 2\n            y1 = yc - bh / 2\n            x2 = xc + bw / 2\n            y2 = yc + bh / 2\n\n            # Keep inside image\n            x1 = max(0, x1)\n            y1 = max(0, y1)\n            x2 = min(width - 1, x2)\n            y2 = min(height - 1, y2)\n\n            boxes.append((x1, y1, x2, y2))\n\n    return boxes","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.621085Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.621397Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.635Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.621329Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.634261Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**`loadmat(...)` in your ShanghaiTech dataset**\n\nLoads a MATLAB .mat file. For ShanghaiTech, the .mat file contains head-point annotations, `GT` represents one point per person, not a bounding box.\n\n**`load_gt_boxes(...)`**\nIt is for YOLO-format .txt annotation files\n\n**Main difference**\n\n| Feature | `loadmat` | `load_gt_boxes` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Annotation file | `.mat` | `.txt` |\n| Format | MATLAB | YOLO |\n| Annotation | Person/head points | Person bounding boxes |\n| Example | `(250, 300)` | `(200, 250, 300, 450)` |\n| Output | Array of points | List of boxes |\n| Used for | Crowd density/counting | Object detection |\n| Gives object size? | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |\n| Gives class? | Usually no useful class ID | ✅ Yes, via YOLO class ID |\n| Directly usable for density map? | ✅ Yes | ❌ Not directly |\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def predict_fourcolumn_mcnn(image_path):\n    image_bgr = cv2.imread(str(image_path))\n\n    if image_bgr is None:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Could not read image: {image_path}\")\n\n    # RGB copy for visualization\n    original_image = cv2.cvtColor(image_bgr, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n\n    # RESIZE EXACTLY LIKE TRAINING\n    resized_image = cv2.resize(original_image, (768, 768))\n    \n    # CONVERT TO PYTORCH TENSOR\n    input_tensor = torch.from_numpy(resized_image.transpose(2, 0, 1)).float() / 255.0\n        \n    # Add batch dimension\n    input_tensor = input_tensor.unsqueeze(0)\n    \n    # Move to device\n    input_tensor = input_tensor.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n    \n    # INFERENCE\n    model.eval()\n    \n    with torch.inference_mode():\n        prediction = model(input_tensor)\n\n    density_map = prediction.squeeze().cpu().numpy()\n\n    # COUNT\n    predicted_count = float(np.sum(density_map))\n    \n    return (original_image, resized_image, density_map, predicted_count)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.636069Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.636401Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.649031Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.636369Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.648409Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Convert the image to a PyTorch tensor\n\nThe original image has shape:\n```\nH × W × C\n768 × 768 × 3\n```\nPyTorch expects image tensors in:\n```\nC × H × W\n3 × 768 × 768\n```\n\n### Add the batch dimension\n\nBefore:\n```\n[3, 768, 768]\n```\nAfter:\n```\n[1, 3, 768, 768]\n```\n\nThe `1` represents the batch size.\n\nSo you're giving the model one image.\n\n\n### Evaluation Mode\n\nThis tells PyTorch that you're doing inference, not training.\n\nIt is important for layers such as:\n\n* Dropout\n* Batch Normalization\n\nbecause their behavior differs between training and evaluation.\n\n### Disable gradient calculation\n\nSince you aren't training the model, you don't need gradients.\n\nThis reduces memory usage and makes inference more efficient.\n\n\n### Extract the density map\n\nSuppose the model produces:\n```\n[1, 1, 192, 192]\n```\n`squeeze()` removes the unnecessary dimensions:\n```\n[192, 192]\n```\n\n**Why `.cpu()` at the end?**  \n\nBecause NumPy cannot directly work with a CUDA tensor.\n\nThis would fail if prediction is on GPU","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import kagglehub\n\ndataset_path = kagglehub.dataset_download(\"masum1834e/public-transport-crowd-counting\")\nprint(\"Dataset downloaded to:\", dataset_path)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.649974Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.650286Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.749049Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.650252Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.748195Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from pathlib import Path\n\nDATASET_ROOT = Path(dataset_path)\n\nIMAGE_DIR = DATASET_ROOT / \"images\"\nLABEL_DIR = DATASET_ROOT / \"labels\"\n\nprint(\"Dataset:\", DATASET_ROOT)\nprint(\"Images:\", IMAGE_DIR)\nprint(\"Labels:\", LABEL_DIR)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.74995Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.750553Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.754832Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.750531Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T20:59:57.754092Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"image_files = sorted(IM